1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,754
<font color="#FFFFFF">What if reaching a goal were just the reverse of a
diffusion process?</font>

2
00:00:04,834 --> 00:00:06,261
<font color="#FFFFFF">In this ICML 2024 paper,</font>

3
00:00:06,341 --> 00:00:11,976
<font color="#FFFFFF">Vineet Jain and Siamak Ravanbakhsh from McGill
University and Mila reframe goal-conditioned</font>

4
00:00:12,056 --> 00:00:15,933
<font color="#FFFFFF">reinforcement learning through the lens of
denoising diffusion.</font>

5
00:00:16,013 --> 00:00:17,691
<font color="#FFFFFF">Their method, called Merlin,</font>

6
00:00:17,771 --> 00:00:23,972
<font color="#FFFFFF">builds trajectories that drift away from goal
states and then trains a policy to reverse that drift,</font>

7
00:00:24,052 --> 00:00:27,677
<font color="#FFFFFF">exactly as a diffusion model denoises noise back
into data.</font>

8
00:00:27,757 --> 00:00:33,644
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result is a value-function-free approach that
reaches goals from arbitrary starting states,</font>

9
00:00:33,724 --> 00:00:37,161
<font color="#FFFFFF">needs only a single denoising step per environment step,</font>

10
00:00:37,241 --> 00:00:43,190
<font color="#FFFFFF">and runs an order of magnitude faster than other
diffusion-based reinforcement learning methods.</font>

11
00:00:43,570 --> 00:00:49,012
<font color="#FFFFFF">Goal-conditioned reinforcement learning aims to
train a single agent that can reach any</font>

12
00:00:49,092 --> 00:00:51,234
<font color="#FFFFFF">target state within an environment,</font>

13
00:00:51,314 --> 00:00:56,185
<font color="#FFFFFF">using only a sparse reward of one when the goal is
reached and zero otherwise.</font>

14
00:00:56,265 --> 00:01:01,327
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the offline setting, the agent must learn purely
from a pre-collected dataset,</font>

15
00:01:01,407 --> 00:01:03,358
<font color="#FFFFFF">without any further interaction.</font>

16
00:01:03,438 --> 00:01:07,738
<font color="#FFFFFF">The trouble is that most methods rely on estimating
a value function,</font>

17
00:01:07,818 --> 00:01:12,118
<font color="#FFFFFF">and in the offline goal-conditioned setting this
estimate is fragile.</font>

18
00:01:12,198 --> 00:01:15,292
<font color="#FFFFFF">Policies generate actions not present in the data,</font>

19
00:01:15,372 --> 00:01:18,339
<font color="#FFFFFF">the value estimates for those actions are wrong,</font>

20
00:01:18,419 --> 00:01:22,274
<font color="#FFFFFF">and these errors compound over time until the
policy diverges.</font>

21
00:01:22,354 --> 00:01:26,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">Sparse binary rewards only make the estimation
problem harder.</font>

22
00:01:26,590 --> 00:01:30,659
<font color="#FFFFFF">Diffusion models have become a powerful class of
generative models.</font>

23
00:01:30,739 --> 00:01:36,169
<font color="#FFFFFF">They work by defining a forward process that
gradually destroys data into Gaussian noise,</font>

24
00:01:36,249 --> 00:01:41,123
<font color="#FFFFFF">then learning a reverse process that denoises noise
back into realistic samples,</font>

25
00:01:41,203 --> 00:01:43,662
<font color="#FFFFFF">without ever estimating a value function.</font>

26
00:01:43,742 --> 00:01:50,225
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors ask a simple question: what if we treat
goal states as the data distribution we want to model?</font>

27
00:01:50,305 --> 00:01:54,064
<font color="#FFFFFF">In a diffusion model, noise walks away from the
data manifold;</font>

28
00:01:54,144 --> 00:01:56,727
<font color="#FFFFFF">in goal-conditioned reinforcement learning,</font>

29
00:01:56,807 --> 00:02:00,813
<font color="#FFFFFF">we can construct trajectories that walk away from
potential goals.</font>

30
00:02:00,893 --> 00:02:06,386
<font color="#FFFFFF">Learning to reverse those deviations is directly
analogous to learning the score function,</font>

31
00:02:06,466 --> 00:02:11,030
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it sidesteps the value-estimation problems that
plague offline methods.</font>

32
00:02:11,410 --> 00:02:14,842
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three main contributions.</font>

33
00:02:14,922 --> 00:02:18,353
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it presents Merlin,</font>

34
00:02:18,433 --> 00:02:21,865
<font color="#FFFFFF">a fresh perspective that casts goal-conditioned
reinforcement learning as a reverse diffusion</font>

35
00:02:21,945 --> 00:02:25,377
<font color="#FFFFFF">process operating directly over the state space of
the environment.</font>

36
00:02:25,457 --> 00:02:28,888
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

37
00:02:28,968 --> 00:02:32,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">it proves that this reverse process can be learned
by simple goal-conditioned behavior</font>

38
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:35,912
<font color="#FFFFFF">cloning with hindsight relabeling,</font>

39
00:02:35,992 --> 00:02:39,423
<font color="#FFFFFF">eliminating the need for a value function entirely.</font>

40
00:02:39,503 --> 00:02:42,935
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, it develops three ways to construct the forward,</font>

41
00:02:43,015 --> 00:02:46,447
<font color="#FFFFFF">goal-departing trajectories: a fixed heuristic version,</font>

42
00:02:46,527 --> 00:02:49,958
<font color="#FFFFFF">a parametric learned forward model called Merlin-P,</font>

43
00:02:50,038 --> 00:02:53,470
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a non-parametric version called Merlin-NP that
stitches together nearby states in a learned latent space.</font>

44
00:02:53,850 --> 00:02:55,319
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here is how Merlin works.</font>

45
00:02:55,399 --> 00:02:59,346
<font color="#FFFFFF">Take a trajectory from the offline dataset and read
it backwards,</font>

46
00:02:59,426 --> 00:03:01,515
<font color="#FFFFFF">starting from its final goal state.</font>

47
00:03:01,595 --> 00:03:08,764
<font color="#FFFFFF">Applying a forward diffusion transformation at each
step produces states that drift progressively away from the goal,</font>

48
00:03:08,844 --> 00:03:12,110
<font color="#FFFFFF">exactly like adding noise in an image diffusion model.</font>

49
00:03:12,190 --> 00:03:17,129
<font color="#FFFFFF">Merlin then trains a goal-conditioned policy to
reverse this drift, step by step,</font>

50
00:03:17,209 --> 00:03:18,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">back toward the goal.</font>

51
00:03:18,510 --> 00:03:23,697
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key theoretical result is that maximizing the
likelihood of the goal states under</font>

52
00:03:23,777 --> 00:03:28,840
<font color="#FFFFFF">this reverse process is equivalent to plain
behavior cloning on the relabeled data,</font>

53
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:31,070
<font color="#FFFFFF">so no value function is ever needed.</font>

54
00:03:31,150 --> 00:03:34,416
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because the state space itself is the diffusion space,</font>

55
00:03:34,496 --> 00:03:38,877
<font color="#FFFFFF">the policy needs only one denoising iteration for
each environment step,</font>

56
00:03:38,957 --> 00:03:42,967
<font color="#FFFFFF">instead of the many iterations typical diffusion
policies require.</font>

57
00:03:43,047 --> 00:03:48,667
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors offer three ways to build the
goal-departing trajectories: a fixed construction,</font>

58
00:03:48,747 --> 00:03:51,579
<font color="#FFFFFF">a learned parametric forward model in Merlin-P,</font>

59
00:03:51,659 --> 00:03:57,279
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a non-parametric model in Merlin-NP that
connects nearby states through nearest-neighbor</font>

60
00:03:57,359 --> 00:03:59,510
<font color="#FFFFFF">stitching in a learned latent space.</font>

61
00:03:59,890 --> 00:04:06,181
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors evaluate Merlin on a standard offline
goal-conditioned benchmark of ten control tasks,</font>

62
00:04:06,261 --> 00:04:12,877
<font color="#FFFFFF">ranging from simple point navigation and reacher
tasks to harder Fetch manipulation tasks like pushing,</font>

63
00:04:12,957 --> 00:04:16,972
<font color="#FFFFFF">picking, and sliding, plus the high-dimensional
HandReach task.</font>

64
00:04:17,052 --> 00:04:22,563
<font color="#FFFFFF">Every task uses a sparse binary reward and a
maximum trajectory length of fifty steps.</font>

65
00:04:22,643 --> 00:04:29,779
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each task comes in two flavors: an expert dataset
collected by a trained policy with added noise for diversity,</font>

66
00:04:29,859 --> 00:04:33,549
<font color="#FFFFFF">and a random dataset collected by sampling random actions.</font>

67
00:04:33,629 --> 00:04:39,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">They test both low-dimensional state observations
and high-dimensional pixel observations,</font>

68
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:42,390
<font color="#FFFFFF">and average all results over ten random seeds.</font>

69
00:04:42,770 --> 00:04:45,283
<font color="#FFFFFF">The results are strong across the board.</font>

70
00:04:45,363 --> 00:04:50,210
<font color="#FFFFFF">The basic version of Merlin already outperforms the
baselines on most tasks,</font>

71
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:53,711
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the two improved variants, Merlin-P and Merlin-NP,</font>

72
00:04:53,791 --> 00:04:58,963
<font color="#FFFFFF">push performance further to achieve the highest
discounted returns on most tasks.</font>

73
00:04:59,043 --> 00:05:02,982
<font color="#FFFFFF">Measured by average rank across all ten methods and
ten tasks,</font>

74
00:05:03,062 --> 00:05:04,733
<font color="#FFFFFF">Merlin-NP comes out on top,</font>

75
00:05:04,813 --> 00:05:12,318
<font color="#FFFFFF">with an average rank around one point seven on
state observations and about one point two five on pixel observations.</font>

76
00:05:12,398 --> 00:05:17,828
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially, because Merlin performs only a single
denoising step per environment step,</font>

77
00:05:17,908 --> 00:05:24,182
<font color="#FFFFFF">its training and inference are roughly an order of
magnitude faster than the other diffusion-based</font>

78
00:05:24,262 --> 00:05:26,775
<font color="#FFFFFF">methods like Decision Diffuser and BESO.</font>

79
00:05:26,855 --> 00:05:29,433
<font color="#FFFFFF">With high-dimensional pixel observations,</font>

80
00:05:29,513 --> 00:05:32,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">that efficiency gap becomes even more pronounced.</font>

81
00:05:32,990 --> 00:05:39,838
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors study two key hyperparameters: the
hindsight relabeling ratio and the evaluation time horizon,</font>

82
00:05:39,918 --> 00:05:42,518
<font color="#FFFFFF">which sets how far ahead the policy aims.</font>

83
00:05:42,598 --> 00:05:47,617
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across all tasks, conditioning on a time horizon
clearly beats leaving it out.</font>

84
00:05:47,697 --> 00:05:55,133
<font color="#FFFFFF">The best value depends on the task: easier tasks
like PointReach do well with a short horizon of one or five steps,</font>

85
00:05:55,213 --> 00:06:01,343
<font color="#FFFFFF">while harder tasks benefit from longer horizons
because they need more steps to reach the goal.</font>

86
00:06:01,423 --> 00:06:04,284
<font color="#FFFFFF">The HandReach task is particularly sensitive,</font>

87
00:06:04,364 --> 00:06:09,448
<font color="#FFFFFF">working dramatically better with a horizon of one
than with any longer setting.</font>

88
00:06:09,528 --> 00:06:16,180
<font color="#FFFFFF">These trends are visualized as heatmaps of returns
across horizons for both expert and random datasets.</font>

89
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:21,770
<font color="#FFFFFF">The big takeaway is that goal-conditioned
reinforcement learning can be reframed as the</font>

90
00:06:21,850 --> 00:06:23,655
<font color="#FFFFFF">reverse of a diffusion process,</font>

91
00:06:23,735 --> 00:06:27,303
<font color="#FFFFFF">and that this reframing makes the problem
remarkably simple.</font>

92
00:06:27,383 --> 00:06:32,411
<font color="#FFFFFF">By constructing trajectories that walk away from
goals and learning to reverse them,</font>

93
00:06:32,491 --> 00:06:35,755
<font color="#FFFFFF">Merlin reduces goal-reaching to plain behavior cloning,</font>

94
00:06:35,835 --> 00:06:39,585
<font color="#FFFFFF">with no value function to estimate and no
instability to fight.</font>

95
00:06:39,665 --> 00:06:43,355
<font color="#FFFFFF">It matches or beats state-of-the-art methods across
ten tasks,</font>

96
00:06:43,435 --> 00:06:47,976
<font color="#FFFFFF">yet runs an order of magnitude faster than other
diffusion-based approaches.</font>

97
00:06:48,056 --> 00:06:52,233
<font color="#FFFFFF">This suggests that diffusion in the state space is
a simple, scalable,</font>

98
00:06:52,313 --> 00:06:55,820
<font color="#FFFFFF">and practical new direction for sequential decision
making.</font>
